---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  name: "DAX Formatter & Linter",
  url: "https://powerbitutorials.com/tools/dax-audit/",
  applicationCategory: "DeveloperApplication",
  operatingSystem: "Any",
  offers: { "@type": "Offer", price: "0", priceCurrency: "USD" },
  description:
    "Free browser-based DAX formatter and linter for Power BI. Formats measures and flags common performance and correctness traps.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="DAX Formatter & Linter"
  description="Paste any DAX measure to format it and review patterns that slow reports down: repeated measures, FILTER over ALL, COUNTROWS(FILTER()), bare division."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/tools/tool.css" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
    <script is:inline src="/tools/dax.js"></script>
  </Fragment>

  <div class="tool-page">
    <div class="wrap">

      <section class="hero">
        <span class="eyebrow">Free tool · no signup</span>
        <h1>Format your DAX — and find what is slowing it down.</h1>
        <p>
          Paste a measure. You get clean formatting plus a review of the patterns
          that quietly cost performance: the same measure evaluated five times,
          <code>SUMX</code> wrapped around <code>FILTER</code>, division that
          breaks on zero, hard-coded dates. The code is parsed in this tab and
          never uploaded.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="layout" aria-label="DAX formatter and linter">

        <div class="panel">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <span>Your DAX</span>
            <span class="spacer"></span>
            <button class="btn btn-ghost" id="sample-btn" type="button">Load example</button>
            <button class="btn btn-ghost" id="clear-btn" type="button">Clear</button>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-body">
            <textarea
              class="code-input"
              id="dax-input"
              spellcheck="false"
              autocapitalize="off"
              autocomplete="off"
              aria-label="DAX code to format and check"
              placeholder="Paste a DAX measure, or a full EVALUATE query…"></textarea>
            <div class="stats" id="stats"></div>
            <p class="note">
              Shortcut: <kbd>⌘</kbd>/<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd> to run.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="panel">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <span>Formatted</span>
              <span class="spacer"></span>
              <button class="btn" id="copy-btn" type="button">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-body">
              <pre class="code-output empty" id="dax-output">// Your formatted DAX will appear here</pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <span>Checks</span>
              <span class="spacer"></span>
              <span class="summary" id="summary">Waiting for input</span>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-body" id="issues"></div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </section>

      <section class="cta">
        <div>
          <h3>Want the checks turned into working measures?</h3>
          <p>
            The free Time Intelligence Starter Pack ships a marked date table and
            twelve measures that already avoid every trap this tool flags.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/resources/time-intelligence-starter/">Get the pack →</a>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="faq-title">How the check works</h2>
        <p class="faq-intro">
          The tool tokenises DAX — strings, comments and quoted table names
          included — then applies rules that can be decided from the syntax
          alone. It is deliberately conservative: when the intent depends on
          runtime data, it stays silent instead of guessing.
        </p>

        <details class="faq">
          <summary>What does it check for?</summary>
          <p>
            Repeated evaluation of the same measure, nested CALCULATE, IFERROR
            around aggregates, SUMX/AVERAGEX over FILTER, COUNTROWS(FILTER(...)),
            bare division without DIVIDE, EARLIER, SUMMARIZE used to add columns,
            hard-coded DATE() literals, FILTER(ALL(...)), AND()/OR() functions,
            and deep nesting.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq">
          <summary>Is my DAX uploaded anywhere?</summary>
          <p>
            No. The formatter and every rule are plain JavaScript running in this
            tab, with no backend call and no account. You can turn off your
            network and reload the page to confirm. The site itself uses
            cookie-consent based analytics for page traffic — your formula is not
            part of that.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq">
          <summary>Does a clean result mean my measure is correct?</summary>
          <p>
            No. These are syntactic heuristics, not a semantic analyser. A measure
            can pass every rule and still return the wrong number. Treat the
            output as a second pair of eyes, not as a test suite.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq">
          <summary>Why not just use DAX Formatter?</summary>
          <p>
            DAX Formatter, by SQLBI, is excellent at formatting and is the
            industry standard — use it if that is all you need. This tool adds a
            review layer aimed at learning: every issue links back to the
            tutorial that explains the pattern.
          </p>
        </details>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>

  <script is:inline src="/tools/dax-audit.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .faq-title { font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 0 0 4px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .faq-intro {
    color: var(--t-text-muted); font-size: 0.94rem; margin: 0 0 8px;
    max-width: 760px; line-height: 1.7;
  }
</style>
